last question today's from nico nico  wrote in and said hey David what's the  most unrealistic Democratic campaign  promise of 2020 so far obviously Donald  Trump all along was making unrealistic  campaign promises but what are  unrealistic ones from the left so  there's two ways to answer this question  there's the micro answer and there's the  macro answer if you want to play the  micro of specific policy there are lots  of policies that are unrealistic in the  sense that they ignore that you would  need as president both the House and  Senate to be democratically controlled  in order to make them a reality almost  guaranteed okay so Medicare for all for  example how do you do Medicare for all  without control of the Senate in 2020  yes it's possible in the hypothetical  sense it's extraordinarily unlikely  there's certain infrastructure stuff  that's not realistic in terms of its  political viability without control of  the House and Senate and you have to  understand that we have many examples  even with control by the same party of  the White House the house in the Senate  it's still hard to do stuff universal  background checks when Barack Obama was  in the White House and controlled the  House and Senate 90% support for that  from the American public still couldn't  get it done gun lobby significantly to  blame there but the point is there are  lots of policies that are effectively  non-starters if you don't control the  White House the Senate and the House of  Representatives but that's the micro I  think them this question is more  interestingly addressed at the macro  level the most unrealistic meta or macro  promise is bipartisanship everybody pays  lip service to bipartisanship I know how  to work with the other side I have a  long history of drafting and sponsoring  legislation with people from the other  side of the aisle I would work together  in ways that other candidates aren't  able to I don't think it's a bad idea  it's just completely unrealistic the  last 30 years of increased polarization  have shown us this I did a story on the  Monday show talking about how Donald  Trump is in practice just a symptom of  the problem which has been the rise in  reactionary politics from the right for  the last 20 to 30 years depending on it  how you want to measure it Republic  won't even call out Trump for relatively  minor things you're not going to get  real bipartisanship in this environment  there's no upside to even trying to get  bipartisanship the way our political  system is designed and you actually risk  your own party turning against you if  you start working with the other side so  I'm not saying I don't want  bipartisanship I'm not saying that the  people promising it are straight-up  lying or don't want it themselves what  I'm telling you is that if you ask me  what's the most unrealistic promise we  hear from Democratic candidates is that  they are going to be able to work with  Republicans if they are elected  Republicans who likely will control the  Senate after 2020 and get stuff done  they will have to get stuff done despite  Republicans I would love to hear them  just say that and that's the most  unrealistic promise that we're hearing  that's it for today we've got a great  bonus show keep sending in questions for  next week become a member or a patron to  get instant access to the bonus show WWE  at medCom  or patreon.com slash david pakman show
 

